[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":37},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-7,"_20":21,"_22":-7,"_23":24,"_25":26},"tipo","etapa","cubo",{"_11":35,"_13":35,"_28":36},"id","centros","nome","Centers","rascunho",false,"html","<p>The 4x4 has 8 corners, 24 edge pieces (12 <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pairs</a>) and 24 center pieces (4 on each face). The idea of <a href=\"/en/glossary#reducao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"For 4x4 and 5x5: build the centers and pair up the edges until the cube is like a 3x3, which is solved the usual way.\">reduction</a> is to turn the 4x4 into a big 3x3: each four-piece center becomes a single center, and each edge pair becomes a single edge. The first step is the centers.</p>\n<div class=\"diagramas\"><figure class=\"diagrama\"><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/c5897bb80cb7d60e.svg\" alt=\"The goal: the 6 centers done, each with 4 pieces of the same color.\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The goal: the 6 centers done, each with 4 pieces of the same color.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<h2>The color order</h2>\n<p>The 4x4 centers have no fixed place: you build each one wherever you like, but in the same order as the 3x3. White is opposite yellow, green opposite blue and red opposite orange. With yellow on top and green in front, orange is on the right and red on the left. If you get the order wrong, nothing will match when you do the edges.</p>\n<h2>The inner moves</h2>\n<p>The centers move with the inner layers: <code>2R</code> turns only the second layer from the right, and <code>Rw</code> turns the two right layers together (see the 4x4 <a href=\"/en/glossary#notacao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The letters that describe the moves: R, U, F… Each cube has its own notation page.\">notation</a>). The outer faces (<code>R</code>, <code>U</code>…) only turn a center in its own place.</p>\n<p>The trick in every step: turn an inner layer to bring pieces to the face you are building, turn that face to store them, and turn the inner layer back. That way, whatever was already done goes back in place.</p>\n<h2>1. The first center: white</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Put two white pieces side by side on one face: a &quot;bar&quot;.</li>\n<li>Make another white bar, on another face or the same one.</li>\n<li>Fit one bar next to the other, turning the inner layer that takes the second bar to the first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This first center comes without an algorithm: turn, look and try again. With practice, it takes a few turns.</p>\n<h2>2. The second center: yellow</h2>\n<p>Hold the cube with white on the bottom and build yellow on top, the same way: bars and the inner layer trick. Careful: an inner layer also takes part of the white along. So always turn the layer back after storing the pieces on top.</p>\n<h2>3. The last 4 centers</h2>\n<p>With white and yellow done, hold the cube with them on the sides (white on the left, yellow on the right). The 4 centers left are on top, in front, on the bottom and at the back. Now <code>2R</code> and <code>2L</code> swap pieces among these 4 centers without touching white or yellow, and <code>U</code> and <code>F</code> only turn centers in place. Build one center at a time, always in the color order, and leave the last pair for the end: the two that remain get done together.</p>\n","treino","metodos",[30],"anterior","proximo",{"_13":27,"_28":29},"grupos",[],"Edges","endereco","/4x4/arestas",{"_13":31,"_32":33,"_28":34},"Reduction","numero",1,"/4x4/reducao#centros","4x4","/4x4/",{"_38":39,"_40":41},"cubos",[44,45,46,47,48,49,50],"idiomas",[42,43],"pt","en",{"_11":59,"_13":59},{"_11":58,"_13":58},{"_11":35,"_13":35},{"_11":57,"_13":57},{"_11":55,"_13":56},{"_11":53,"_13":54},{"_11":51,"_13":52},"skewb","Skewb","pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","5x5","2x2","3x3"]
